import "core"
import "core"


# Partial application, as `docs/philosophy/basics.md` describes it: a name may be bound to a
  call that has only some of its arguments, and the rest are applied later.
    baseFunctionCall = callName(arg1=1, arg2=2)
    finalCall = baseFunctionCall(arg3=9)
  `g` is `f` with `b` and `c` already given, so `g(a= 1)` is `f(a= 1, b= 2, c= 3)`.
  The arguments `g` carries have to survive the later application: applying `a` extends the
  tuple `g` already is, rather than starting a fresh one. Distinct non-zero values, so the
  answer distinguishes an argument that landed in the wrong slot.
f(a -> Integer, b -> Integer, c -> Integer) -> Integer= a + b + c

g= f(b= 2, c= 3)

main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= g(a= 1) == 6)
  io
